# AWS Load Balancers: ALB vs NLB vs GWLB

## What I Learned Today

Today I explored the **three types of AWS Load Balancers** — Application Load Balancer (ALB), Network Load Balancer (NLB), and Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) — and when to use each one. The session also covered the foundational concepts of what a load balancer is and how traffic flows through the OSI model layers.

---

## What is a Load Balancer?

When a single server cannot handle the volume of incoming requests, multiple server instances are deployed. A **load balancer** sits in front of these instances and distributes incoming traffic across them.

```
          Users (100 requests)
                  ↓
           Load Balancer
          ↙      ↓      ↘
      EC2-1    EC2-2    EC2-3
    (~33 req) (~33 req) (~33 req)
```

### Benefits
- **High availability** — no single point of failure.
- **Scalability** — add more instances as traffic grows.
- **Better user experience** — eliminates slowness and downtime caused by overloaded servers.

### Common Load Balancing Techniques
- **Round Robin** — requests distributed evenly across all instances in order.
- **Weighted / Ratio-based** — more powerful instances receive more requests.
- **Sticky Sessions** — all requests from the same user go to the same server (important for stateful workloads).

---

## The OSI Model — Why It Matters for Load Balancers

Different load balancers operate at different OSI layers. Understanding which layer a load balancer acts on determines what it can and cannot do.

| Layer | Name | What Happens Here |
|---|---|---|
| **7** | Application | Protocol selection — HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, etc. |
| **6** | Presentation | Encryption/decryption — SSL/TLS |
| **5** | Session | Session management between client and server |
| **4** | Transport | Data split into packets; TCP/UDP; reliable transmission |
| **3** | Network | Routing through routers across the internet |
| **2** | Data Link | Switches; local network delivery |
| **1** | Physical | Cables, hardware; actual data transmission |

### How a Request Travels (Example: Accessing a Website)

```
User opens browser → HTTP request created (Layer 7)
       ↓
TLS encryption applied (Layer 6)
       ↓
Session established (Layer 5)
       ↓
Request split into TCP packets (Layer 4)
       ↓
Packets routed through internet routers (Layer 3)
       ↓
Switch in data center receives packets (Layer 2)
       ↓
Cable delivers to server hardware (Layer 1)
       ↓
Server processes and responds
```

**Key rule:** The layer at which a load balancer operates determines what information it can read and act upon.

---

## The Three AWS Load Balancer Types

| Load Balancer | OSI Layer | Protocol | Primary Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| **ALB** — Application | Layer 7 | HTTP / HTTPS | Web apps, microservices, path/host-based routing |
| **NLB** — Network | Layer 4 | TCP / UDP | Streaming, gaming, high-performance, low-latency |
| **GWLB** — Gateway | Layer 3+4 | All traffic | Virtual appliances — firewalls, VPN, intrusion detection |

---

## Application Load Balancer (ALB) — Layer 7

### How It Works
ALB intercepts the HTTP/HTTPS request at **Layer 7** before forwarding it. Because it can read the full HTTP request, it can make routing decisions based on:

- **URL path** — e.g., `/payments` → Payments Service, `/login` → Auth Service
- **Host header** — e.g., `api.example.com` vs `www.example.com`
- **HTTP headers** — custom routing based on request metadata
- **Query strings** — route based on URL parameters
- **Weighted routing** — send X% of traffic to version A, Y% to version B (useful for canary deployments)

### Example: Path-Based Routing

```
User → ALB (amazon.com)
         ├── /payments   → Payments Target Group (EC2s)
         ├── /login      → Auth Target Group (EC2s)
         └── /transactions → Transactions Target Group (EC2s)
```

### Additional Capabilities
- **SSL Offloading** — ALB terminates SSL from the client; sends plain HTTP to backend servers (or re-encrypts).
- **SSL Pass-Through** — forwards encrypted traffic directly to the backend.
- **Authentication** — integrates with Cognito or OIDC providers.
- **Web Application Firewall (WAF)** — can be attached directly to ALB.

### Trade-offs
- **Cost:** Higher than NLB — more features, more processing.
- **Latency:** Slightly slower — ALB reads and inspects each request before forwarding.

### When to Use ALB
- Microservices that need path or host-based routing.
- Web applications serving HTTP/HTTPS traffic.
- When advanced HTTP routing, SSL offloading, or authentication is needed.

---

## Network Load Balancer (NLB) — Layer 4

### How It Works
NLB operates at **Layer 4 (Transport Layer)** and routes traffic based on **IP address and TCP/UDP port** — without reading HTTP request content. It forwards TCP/UDP packets with minimal processing.

### Key Characteristics
- **Ultra-low latency** — no request inspection, fastest possible forwarding.
- **High throughput** — handles millions of requests per second.
- **Static IP support** — NLB can be assigned a fixed IP address per availability zone (ALB cannot).
- **Sticky Sessions** — all packets from the same client connection go to the same backend server (critical for stateful protocols).
- **Preserves source IP** — the backend server sees the client's real IP address.

### Why Sticky Sessions Matter for Streaming

```
User watching a 3-hour movie:
  Packet 1 → NLB → Server A  ✅
  Packet 2 → NLB → Server A  ✅ (sticky)
  Packet 3 → NLB → Server A  ✅ (sticky)

Without sticky sessions (ALB routing packets independently):
  Packet 1 → ALB → Server A
  Packet 2 → ALB → Server B  ❌ (video breaks — different server, no state)
```

### Trade-offs
- **No HTTP-level routing** — cannot route based on URL path or headers.
- **Lower cost** than ALB.

### When to Use NLB
- Video/content streaming platforms (YouTube-like).
- Online gaming servers.
- Any workload where **latency** is critical and HTTP-level routing is not needed.
- Applications requiring **static IP** on the load balancer.

---

## Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) — Layer 3/4

### What It Is
GWLB is designed specifically for deploying, scaling, and managing **third-party virtual network appliances** such as:

- **Firewalls**
- **VPN gateways**
- **Intrusion Detection / Prevention Systems (IDS/IPS)**
- **Deep packet inspection tools**

### How It Differs from ALB and NLB
- GWLB routes **all traffic** (not just HTTP or TCP/UDP) through virtual appliances for inspection or security processing.
- It sends **highly encrypted packets** to the virtual appliances — a level of security that ALB and NLB cannot provide.
- It uses the **GENEVE protocol** to encapsulate traffic, preserving all original packet information while routing through the appliance.

### Architecture

```
Internet Traffic
      ↓
Gateway Load Balancer
      ↓
Virtual Appliance (Firewall / IDS)
[Inspect / Filter / Encrypt]
      ↓
Backend Application Servers
```

### When to Use GWLB
- Building or operating **security appliance products** (firewall vendors, VPN providers).
- Organizations that need **all traffic inspected** by a third-party security tool before it reaches application servers.
- Any use case involving virtual network appliances at scale.

---

## Decision Guide — Which Load Balancer to Choose

```
Do you need to inspect HTTP traffic and route based on path/host/headers?
    └── YES → ALB (Application Load Balancer)

Do you need ultra-low latency, TCP/UDP routing, or static IPs?
    └── YES → NLB (Network Load Balancer)

Are you deploying virtual network appliances (firewalls, VPN, IDS)?
    └── YES → GWLB (Gateway Load Balancer)
```

### Quick Reference Table

| Requirement | ALB | NLB | GWLB |
|---|---|---|---|
| HTTP path-based routing | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Host-based routing | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Ultra-low latency | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Video streaming | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Gaming servers | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Static IP on LB | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Sticky sessions | Limited | ✅ | ✅ |
| Firewall / VPN appliances | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| SSL offloading | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Cost | High | Medium | Medium |

---

## Key Concepts Summary

| Concept | Description |
|---|---|
| **Load Balancer** | Distributes traffic across multiple servers to ensure high availability and scalability |
| **Round Robin** | Basic LB technique — requests distributed evenly across servers in order |
| **Sticky Session** | All requests from one client go to the same server — critical for stateful workloads |
| **ALB** | Layer 7 — reads HTTP requests; routes by path, host, headers; best for microservices and web apps |
| **NLB** | Layer 4 — routes by IP/port; ultra-low latency; best for streaming and gaming |
| **GWLB** | Layer 3/4 — routes all traffic through virtual appliances; best for firewalls and VPN products |
| **SSL Offloading** | ALB terminates SSL from the client; reduces encryption overhead on backend servers |
| **OSI Layer 7** | Application layer — HTTP, HTTPS, FTP protocols |
| **OSI Layer 4** | Transport layer — TCP, UDP; packet-level routing |

---
